[SPEAKER_00]: Like the, the goal, the goal of the economic system is to go ahead and say that it's like, oh, well in a world where you are actually not optimizing for things where you're organizing production, not based on markets and are organizing production based on some other thing.

42:39.167 --> 42:40.128
[SPEAKER_00]: how would that work?

42:41.008 --> 42:44.409
[SPEAKER_01]: But that some other thing is still going to be a symbol.

42:45.570 --> 42:50.692
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, that some other thing is almost... Or a system of symbols.

42:50.732 --> 42:53.033
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it like, oh, but that's some other thing.

42:53.673 --> 43:06.358
[SPEAKER_00]: But kind of my point is that if you're doing an economic theory, if you're trying to figure out... At some point in time, you can kind of think about it as the economy as kind of existing in layers of abstraction.

43:07.177 --> 43:10.559
[SPEAKER_00]: you have like the raw physical production processes themselves.

43:11.880 --> 43:15.942
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you have the markets that coordinate those raw production processes.

43:17.402 --> 43:19.444
[SPEAKER_00]: You have the markets that coordinate those raw production processes.

43:20.744 --> 43:24.886
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sorry, firms and markets that coordinate those raw production processes.

43:25.187 --> 43:28.008
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you have like interactions between firms that will go ahead and cause a bunch of things.

43:28.028 --> 43:30.409
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you have like state regulation that exists on top of that.

43:30.710 --> 43:35.292
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you have like very large markets, like kind of like the global US market for labor in a very abstract way.

43:37.418 --> 43:47.449
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you also have, like, all of these differing, like, these bickering groups between different groups of capital at the very top that kind of control everything.

43:47.469 --> 43:52.675
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you also kind of have, like, the, and then you can argue that, like, the political system is the thing that exists at, like, the highest level of...

43:53.870 --> 43:58.891
[SPEAKER_00]: The political system is the thing that exists at the highest level of hierarchy and is determined by every single thing below it.

43:59.512 --> 44:08.634
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem with economic models is that the only thing that actually is staying the same, or most of what's staying the same, is the underlying production processes themselves.

44:08.674 --> 44:19.177
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're trying to build a good socialist system, you're trying to figure out what abstractions, what is the second organizational layer that you build on top of the raw production processes that results in the most well-being.

44:19.197 --> 44:20.681
[SPEAKER_00]: because you can't really change the raw.

44:21.202 --> 44:21.563
[SPEAKER_00]: It's hard.

44:21.823 --> 44:25.332
[SPEAKER_00]: Like just saying, just change the raw production processes is not economic management.

44:25.352 --> 44:26.756
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like technological development.

44:28.435 --> 44:33.019
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can change the social organizations that determine what forms of production actually get made.

44:33.780 --> 44:46.049
[SPEAKER_00]: And the worry about this is that, like, just kind of, like, thermodynamics doesn't necessarily... Like, thermodynamics does not necessarily tell you about strong... About, like, gluon...

44:46.289 --> 44:50.773
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact that, like, gluon fields interact with each other in a non-abelian way...

44:52.859 --> 45:01.527
